# 6. Reusable Template Architecture

Design the pipeline so that common functionality can be reused across multiple projects in the future.

Separate:

- Pipeline orchestration.
- Reusable job templates.
- Project-specific job configuration.
- Build and deployment scripts.
- Shared configuration.

Recommended structure:

```text
.gitlab-ci.yml
.gitlab/
  ci/
    templates/
      common.yml
      node.yml
      blackduck.yml
      sonar.yml
      jfrog.yml
      sbom.yml
      docker.yml
    jobs/
      prepare.yml
      build.yml
      quality.yml
      package.yml
      container.yml
scripts/
  prepare/
  build/
  security/
  package/
  docker/
```

Adapt the structure to the actual repository.

Do not create unnecessary files or abstraction layers.

## Template Requirements

Use:

- `include:local` for local reusable configurations.
- Hidden jobs with names beginning with `.`.
- `extends` for reusable job configuration.
- `variables` for project-specific configuration.
- `default` where appropriate.
- `stages` for logical pipeline organization.
- `needs` for explicit job dependencies.
- `artifacts` for transferring required build outputs.
- `cache` only for reusable dependency caches.
- `rules` only when necessary.

Evaluate GitLab CI/CD components and `spec:inputs` only if the target GitLab version supports them and they offer a meaningful advantage.

Do not mix too many reuse mechanisms unnecessarily.

## Template Reusability

Templates should not hardcode DevStack-specific values unless unavoidable.

Use configurable variables for:

- Node.js version.
- Build command.
- Artifact directories.
- SonarQube project configuration.
- Black Duck project configuration.
- JFrog repository and artifact paths.
- SBOM output path and format.
- Docker image name.
- Docker image tag.
- Docker registry.
- Runner tags.

Keep secrets in GitLab CI/CD variables or an approved secrets manager.

Do not hardcode credentials.

